{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>You shall not oppress a hired worker who is poor and needy.</b> Scripture speaks in the present tense, for the poor and the needy and the sojourner are the ones who hire themselves out. And similarly, <i>If you lend money to My people, to the poor person who is with you</i> (Exodus 22:24), and similarly, <i>It shall be for the sojourner, the orphan, and the widow</i> (Deuteronomy 24:19) — for they are the ones who are poor in the majority. And similarly in many places Scripture speaks in the present tense, such as <i>You shall not muzzle an ox while it threshes</i> (Deuteronomy 25:4), <i>You shall not plow with an ox and a donkey together</i> (Deuteronomy 22:10). And in the Sifrei (Tetzeh 145) they said: if so, why are \"poor and needy\" stated? — I am quick to pay retribution on account of a poor and needy person more than on account of any other person. And they expounded from <i>from your midst</i> — this refers to a righteous proselyte, to transgress thereby two negative prohibitions."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:11.349372221Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}